    "title": {
      "en": "Blizzard Will Be Taking Action Against Gold Buyers - Potential for Bans",
      "es": "Blizzard tomará medidas contra los compradores de oro - Posibilidad de prohibiciones"
    },
    "summary": {
      "en": "Blizzard Will Be Taking Action Against Gold Buyers - Potential for Bans...",
      "es": "Blizzard tomará medidas contra los compradores de oro: potencial de prohibiciones..."
    },
    "contentHtml": "\n            <div id=\"news-post-6abafffad7bcc0.95433207\" data-insert-ads=\"article\" data-zaf-dynamic=\"article\" data-units-dynamic=\"article\" data-zaf-slot=\"true\">Blizzard has announced that while they have always actioned RMT seller accounts, they will increasingly be taking action against buyers' accounts. <br><br>In response to players discussing streamers with mysterious amounts of gold, Blizzard has reminded players of the rules about Real money transactions, especially in WoW: Forever. They note that they have and will continue to always action RMT seller accounts, but they are increasingly taking action against buyer accounts as well. They are looking to remove the illicit gold and potentially take further action, which could include account bans. <br><br>They remind all players to be careful about accepting gifts from strangers.<div class=\"quote-box exclude-units last\" data-type=\"blizzard\" data-has-links=\"true\" data-has-source=\"true\" data-has-toggle=\"true\"><div class=\"quote-source\">Blizzard</div><div class=\"quote-header\"><a href=\"https://us.forums.blizzard.com/en/wow/t/2364933/43\" target=\"_blank\">View Original</a><a class=\"quote-toggle\" href=\"javascript:\" aria-label=\"Toggle quote visibility\"></a></div><div class=\"quote-body\" data-markup-content-target=\"1\">Hello. We just want to reiterate some key points about this subject.<br><br>Real money transactions (RMT) are not acceptable in World of Warcraft, and we’re taking steps to address RMT in the WoW: Forever Beta, and of course, when WoW: Forever goes live and beyond.<br><br>It’s important to know that while we have always actioned RMT <b>seller’s</b> accounts as quickly as possible, we will increasingly take action against <b>buyers’</b> accounts. That means that we will remove the illicit gold from the recipient, and/<wbr>or take further actions, which can include closing accounts.<br><br>We’re once again asking all players to be very cautious when it comes to accepting gifts from strangers or other one-sided transactions. If it becomes apparent that a recipient knew or should-have-known that gold wasn’t from a legitimate source, they may be subject to account actions as well.</div></div></div>\n            \n            <noscript>Blizzard has announced that while they have always actioned RMT seller accounts, they will increasingly be taking action against buyers' accounts. <br /><br />In response to players discussing streamers with mysterious amounts of gold, Blizzard has reminded players of the rules about Real money transactions, especially in WoW: Forever. They note that they have and will continue to always action RMT seller accounts, but they are increasingly taking action against buyer accounts as well. They are looking to remove the illicit gold and potentially take further action, which could include account bans. <br /><br />They remind all players to be careful about accepting gifts from strangers.<br /><br /><div class=\"quote-box\" data-type=\"blizzard\"><div class=\"quote-header\"></div><div class=\"quote-body\">Hello. We just want to reiterate some key points about this subject.<br /><br />Real money transactions (RMT) are not acceptable in World of Warcraft, and we’re taking steps to address RMT in the WoW: Forever Beta, and of course, when WoW: Forever goes live and beyond.<br /><br />It’s important to know that while we have always actioned RMT <b>seller’s</b> accounts as quickly as possible, we will increasingly take action against <b>buyers’</b> accounts. That means that we will remove the illicit gold from the recipient, and/or take further actions, which can include closing accounts.<br /><br />We’re once again asking all players to be very cautious when it comes to accepting gifts from strangers or other one-sided transactions. If it becomes apparent that a recipient knew or should-have-known that gold wasn’t from a legitimate source, they may be subject to account actions as well.</div></div></noscript>            \n        ",
    "imageUrl": "https://wow.zamimg.com/uploads/blog/images/55514-blizzard-will-be-taking-action-against-gold-buyers-potential-for-bans.jpg",
